Local native compiler and runtime support for Mentec
===================================================

crt0-stack.s and csv-stack.s derive from the host's 2.11BSD libc sources:
    /usr/src/lib/libc/pdp/csu/crt0.s
    /usr/src/lib/libc/pdp/crt/csv.s
DEFS.h is copied from /usr/src/lib/libc/pdp/crt/DEFS.h.
The original copyright notices are retained.

The compiled shell has a largest immediate stack allocation of 1038
bytes; the compiler snapshots have maxima of 3072 (c0) and 2048 (c1).
These are below the 4096-byte prefault interval.

The additions prefault 4096 bytes with sub/tst/add (or restore sp at
startup). A plain memory-access fault grows the stack before a call can
cross the boundary. The csv additions run after saved registers and the
overlay number have been recorded and retain the standard frame layout.

from-host/ contains a snapshot of /usr/src/lib/ccom source files and its
existing native compiler object files. They bootstrap the local passes
without needing a reliable compiler to build a replacement compiler.
The object files are input artifacts, not temporary ksh build outputs.
Their source files are retained for inspection and future rebuilding.
The Makefile deliberately disables implicit rules so the snapshot object
files are not accidentally rebuilt by the affected stock compiler.

make builds the runtime objects using the native preprocessor/assembler
and relinks c0 (-i) and c1 (-n) with the native linker and libc. make clean
removes only these generated helpers, never the bootstrap snapshot.
The separate-I/D choice differs intentionally: c1's upstream table.o has
its instruction-selection data in text and requires the shared format.

No files in /lib, /bin, or /usr/src are changed. The top-level Makefile
selects these private passes and links ksh with the same local runtime.
